WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.085 --> 00:00:05.505
Riley: The world’s oceans make up the
largest habitat on the planet.

00:00:05.797 --> 00:00:08.633
And they contain one of our most
vital sources of food.

00:00:10.218 --> 00:00:10.969
Fish.

00:00:11.428 --> 00:00:13.930
And we eat a lot of it.

00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:29.988
The average human being consumes
around 45 pounds of fish every year.

00:00:30.321 --> 00:00:33.992
And for over 3 billion people, it's
their primary source of protein.

00:00:34.242 --> 00:00:38.079
Global demand for seafood is
rising, fuelling a multibillion-dollar

00:00:38.079 --> 00:00:39.372
commercial fishing industry.

00:00:39.497 --> 00:00:42.667
But experts warn that in some
places, these commercial vessels

00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:44.878
are catching more fish than the
ocean can provide,

00:00:45.211 --> 00:00:49.215
threatening sea life, and the very
people who rely on the ocean the most.

00:00:58.308 --> 00:01:02.103
According to the United Nations,
there are over 370 million

00:01:02.103 --> 00:01:03.897
indigenous people around the world.

00:01:04.564 --> 00:01:08.193
Along the world's coastlines, there
are more than 27 million indigenous

00:01:08.193 --> 00:01:11.029
people spread across at 
least 87 countries.

00:01:11.446 --> 00:01:14.115
And for many of these coastal
communities, the ocean has been

00:01:14.115 --> 00:01:17.660
vital to their culture and survival
for 1000s of years.

00:01:18.036 --> 00:01:21.206
Andrés: So coastal indigenous
peoples is a group that really

00:01:21.206 --> 00:01:24.626
encompasses a lot of different
types of communities, sometimes

00:01:24.626 --> 00:01:25.627
entire nations.

00:01:25.710 --> 00:01:28.338
It's something that's not an
official definition. It's something

00:01:28.338 --> 00:01:32.217
that we use sort of as a shorthand
for indigenous communities that

00:01:32.509 --> 00:01:34.928
still live on coastlines.

00:01:35.053 --> 00:01:39.557
One defining aspect that we see
again and again, is an incredibly

00:01:39.599 --> 00:01:41.059
close relationship with seafood.

00:01:41.476 --> 00:01:44.646
Just the practice of fishing has a
lot of cultural significance, right.

00:01:44.646 --> 00:01:48.399
So even though, yes, they
rely a lot on fish for local food

00:01:48.399 --> 00:01:49.526
security and nutrition,

00:01:49.984 --> 00:01:53.404
even just the practice of going out
is a way in which they share

00:01:53.404 --> 00:01:57.534
stories, communicate knowledge and
really kind of further their traditions.

00:02:04.249 --> 00:02:07.752
Marilyn: The Heiltsuk Nation is one
of the largest tribes on the

00:02:07.752 --> 00:02:08.837
Central Coast.

00:02:09.462 --> 00:02:15.009
We are a marine community. We're
located, you know, right, you know,

00:02:15.426 --> 00:02:17.220
the ocean is right in front of us.

00:02:17.345 --> 00:02:22.517
We live in a really remote area,
and it's very isolated. And it's

00:02:22.517 --> 00:02:25.520
very costly to get, you know,
groceries up here.

00:02:25.812 --> 00:02:31.442
So our sustenance and our way of
life has been to harvest salmon,

00:02:31.484 --> 00:02:35.280
spawn on kelp, crabs, 
prawns, seaweed.

00:02:35.446 --> 00:02:37.866
Our breadbasket is the ocean.

00:02:45.248 --> 00:02:51.004
Dresy: Typically a Vezo is a man or
woman, but mostly men who go

00:02:51.004 --> 00:02:55.842
fishing using the traditional canoe
and who live mainly on fishing.

00:02:56.050 --> 00:02:59.304
The Vezo has been there for centuries.

00:02:59.304 --> 00:03:04.893
and they've been transmitting their 
techniques from generation to generation.

00:03:05.143 --> 00:03:11.649
The sea is, for the Vezo, I think,
the main source of livelihoods and

00:03:11.649 --> 00:03:13.401
also income for the family.

00:03:13.568 --> 00:03:16.321
The simplest one will use the
simplest gears.

00:03:16.946 --> 00:03:21.743
That could be spears and maybe
pulling line, while the rich ones

00:03:21.784 --> 00:03:25.914
can go and buy nets and they'll be
using those nets.

00:03:26.873 --> 00:03:30.335
Depending on where they are on the
coastline, they can catch like

00:03:30.335 --> 00:03:37.383
octopus around the reef. Also, they
can catch squid, tuna, lobster.

00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:40.303
Mostly they're catching 
small pelagic fish.

00:03:45.058 --> 00:03:48.770
Riley: Today, coastal indigenous
people consume around 15 times more

00:03:48.770 --> 00:03:50.563
fish than non-indigenous people.

00:03:50.730 --> 00:03:53.775
And many rely heavily on seafood 
as a source of income. 

00:03:53.775 --> 00:03:57.111
But in recent years, they've been forced 
to compete with large scale commercial

00:03:57.111 --> 00:04:00.949
fishing vessels capable of catching
several tons of fish per day.

00:04:01.449 --> 00:04:06.120
Andrés: Commercial fisheries, at an
industrial scale, are relatively

00:04:06.120 --> 00:04:06.913
recent, right.

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:10.250
Obviously, we've been fishing for a
long, long time, but when we really

00:04:10.250 --> 00:04:15.046
see fisheries sort of globally,
ramp up is, you know, from the 1950s.

00:04:15.672 --> 00:04:18.841
Narrator: British Columbia home,
the world's largest salmon fleets,

00:04:18.967 --> 00:04:22.512
hits the fisherman's jackpot. 
A $5 million catch.

00:04:22.929 --> 00:04:27.267
Nets one third of a mile long are
dropped around a school of fish and

00:04:27.267 --> 00:04:29.018
the hauling in begins.

00:04:29.102 --> 00:04:31.813
Andrés: There's really two
different main kinds. One is the

00:04:31.896 --> 00:04:34.774
national industrial vessels, right
so a lot of countries have their

00:04:34.774 --> 00:04:37.110
national commercial fishing fleets.

00:04:37.110 --> 00:04:39.904
And that often is kind of the first
source of conflict right, you have

00:04:39.904 --> 00:04:43.574
these larger boats, they're
operating locally, but they have

00:04:43.574 --> 00:04:46.744
much more capacity than what
indigenous communities have right

00:04:46.744 --> 00:04:48.830
and so that's a big source of
competition.

00:04:49.247 --> 00:04:52.709
Another one that's relatively more
recent are distant water fisheries.

00:04:52.875 --> 00:04:57.422
These are primarily from 
Eastern Asia, Western Europe.

00:04:57.588 --> 00:05:01.551
These are fleets that go all over
the world. And they have enormous

00:05:01.551 --> 00:05:04.804
impacts not only for indigenous
peoples, but also for just the

00:05:04.804 --> 00:05:06.055
countries where they're operating.

00:05:06.472 --> 00:05:10.435
Probably the most direct way that
this has impacted is obviously

00:05:10.435 --> 00:05:13.229
through overfishing of their shared
stocks, but also through crowding

00:05:13.229 --> 00:05:17.608
out of what used to be traditional
indigenous areas, places where we

00:05:17.608 --> 00:05:22.113
saw these traditional area-based
protections or particular rules

00:05:22.113 --> 00:05:24.657
about when to fish and how and what.

00:05:25.033 --> 00:05:27.952
A lot of these commercial vessels
don't have any respect for that

00:05:27.952 --> 00:05:31.956
sort of thing. And so that has
caused a lot of conflict and

00:05:31.956 --> 00:05:32.456
tension.

00:05:34.417 --> 00:05:36.336
Riley: According to a United Nations report,

00:05:43.801 --> 00:05:47.013
While estimates vary, it's believed
that around one in every five fish

00:05:47.013 --> 00:05:50.558
sold are caught by vessels engaged
in illegal, unreported and

00:05:50.558 --> 00:05:52.769
unregulated fishing activity each year.

00:05:52.977 --> 00:05:54.979
Rachel: There's a significant
amount of noncompliance out there

00:05:54.979 --> 00:05:57.940
globally, and not just distant
water fleets, just generally

00:05:57.940 --> 00:05:59.650
throughout the fishing industry.

00:05:59.859 --> 00:06:03.821
You could have minor offenses up to
what we call serious infringements.

00:06:04.072 --> 00:06:06.866
A minor offense could be 
a mistake on a logbook, 

00:06:06.866 --> 00:06:11.329
up to a serious infringement, we could 
be significant fishing over quota,

00:06:11.954 --> 00:06:14.916
without a license, without
authorization.

00:06:15.041 --> 00:06:18.669
And those are generally the dark
vessels that we talk about, which

00:06:18.669 --> 00:06:20.797
can do significant damage and harm.

00:06:20.922 --> 00:06:23.508
Penalties and sanctions for
fisheries offenses can generally

00:06:23.508 --> 00:06:24.675
tend to be quite low.

00:06:24.967 --> 00:06:27.011
And if you're making millions and
millions of pounds in illegal

00:06:27.011 --> 00:06:31.682
fishing, it's worth breaking the
law. And there are some organized

00:06:31.682 --> 00:06:35.978
fisheries crime syndicates that
will factor these types of fines

00:06:35.978 --> 00:06:39.565
into their activity, almost 
as if it's a business cost.

00:06:40.024 --> 00:06:43.111
Riley: Many of these vessels use a
variety of techniques to skirt the

00:06:43.111 --> 00:06:44.529
law and avoid detection.

00:06:44.737 --> 00:06:47.698
But modern technology is helping
governments and coastal communities

00:06:47.740 --> 00:06:49.826
protect their waters from 
this type of fishing.

00:06:53.162 --> 00:06:55.957
Rachel: So this is done through the
use of remote sensing, so satellite

00:06:55.957 --> 00:06:56.666
technology.

00:06:56.833 --> 00:06:59.961
We use a varying range of sensors
with a different degree of

00:06:59.961 --> 00:07:03.965
resolution. And they help us to
detect vessels that either fish at

00:07:03.965 --> 00:07:07.927
night by detecting their lights or
fishing during the day.

00:07:08.344 --> 00:07:11.973
And we can task and focus these
satellites in areas of interest.

00:07:12.014 --> 00:07:14.976
The intelligence information that
comes from that is then given to

00:07:14.976 --> 00:07:19.814
our partners in government, and
they use that to task their patrols

00:07:19.814 --> 00:07:22.483
and surveillance at sea for
fisheries enforcement.

00:07:22.900 --> 00:07:25.069
One of the problems with vessels on
the high seas is that they're

00:07:25.069 --> 00:07:28.906
technically a piece of that
nation's land at sea.

00:07:29.073 --> 00:07:32.410
Let's just say it's a Madagascar
flagged vessel, and it's a UK

00:07:32.410 --> 00:07:35.163
fisheries protection vessel, you
would need to get permission from

00:07:35.204 --> 00:07:37.957
the Madagascan authorities to board
that vessel in high seas.

00:07:38.499 --> 00:07:42.253
And so some of the techniques are a
constant changing of flag, which

00:07:42.253 --> 00:07:44.714
means that you never know which
nation you're supposed to be asking

00:07:44.714 --> 00:07:46.799
permission, or you'll go to one and
it turns out, they've moved to

00:07:46.799 --> 00:07:48.885
another at which point you're
running out of fuel, you've got to

00:07:48.885 --> 00:07:50.428
go, you're hundreds of miles from land.

00:07:50.887 --> 00:07:57.310
The true dark vessels, the IUU
vessels will use fixers in ports

00:07:57.310 --> 00:07:59.270
so they can land their illegal catches.

00:07:59.353 --> 00:08:03.566
We see all sorts so misreporting of
catches; I say I caught it here and

00:08:03.566 --> 00:08:06.027
then we've actually seen them
fishing somewhere completely differently.

00:08:06.152 --> 00:08:09.947
We see it a lot on boundaries, a
territorial waters boundary, for

00:08:09.947 --> 00:08:12.074
example, but also on marine
protected areas.

00:08:12.283 --> 00:08:14.577
There are buffer zones around
marine protected areas, and you

00:08:14.577 --> 00:08:18.331
will see a vessel sitting right on
the outside of that pointing in a

00:08:18.331 --> 00:08:21.209
certain direction that you can be
pretty confident that the net is

00:08:21.209 --> 00:08:22.919
probably inside the area.

00:08:23.044 --> 00:08:26.839
Often the key thing is they will
switch off their tracking system.

00:08:26.839 --> 00:08:29.717
So this is the AIS, the Automatic
Identification System,

00:08:30.176 --> 00:08:34.347
or they'll spoof, so they can put
another vessel’s AIS system on their

00:08:34.347 --> 00:08:37.558
vessel, or they can actually name
themselves something completely

00:08:37.558 --> 00:08:38.100
different.

00:08:38.851 --> 00:08:43.481
It's not necessarily that these
vessels are not licensed or

00:08:43.481 --> 00:08:46.275
authorized. And there have been
some countries in the past that

00:08:46.275 --> 00:08:49.278
have made a significant amount of
money from opening up their

00:08:49.278 --> 00:08:50.071
licenses.

00:08:50.363 --> 00:08:53.199
And so there are various states,
flags of convenience they're

00:08:53.199 --> 00:08:56.702
called, which will open up their
licenses and their waters to these

00:08:56.702 --> 00:08:59.497
vessels without consideration of
the smaller communities that rely

00:08:59.497 --> 00:09:00.665
on the water and the fish.

00:09:06.879 --> 00:09:10.007
Andrés: The reason that a lot of
these fisheries, the distant water

00:09:10.007 --> 00:09:14.428
fisheries, are able to operate far
from their home ports is because of

00:09:14.428 --> 00:09:15.429
government subsidies.

00:09:15.721 --> 00:09:18.516
These are government's paying for
fuel, for bigger boats, for

00:09:18.516 --> 00:09:19.141
maintenance.

00:09:19.267 --> 00:09:22.562
There was one recent study that
found that if you took those

00:09:22.562 --> 00:09:27.733
subsidies away, about 50% of those
distant water fleets wouldn't be

00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:28.442
profitable.

00:09:28.609 --> 00:09:32.780
Rachel: Most countries, if not all
countries subsidize their fisheries in some way.

00:09:33.072 --> 00:09:36.576
There will be fishing vessels that
will be fishing in a noncompliant

00:09:36.576 --> 00:09:38.828
manner that are in receipt of subsidies.

00:09:41.038 --> 00:09:43.833
Dresy: There are signs of overexploitation.

00:09:44.166 --> 00:09:47.587
Dresy: We have testimony from the
Vezo people saying that, you know,

00:09:47.587 --> 00:09:52.466
once those big vessels are around,
there's no more left to fish, and

00:09:52.466 --> 00:09:56.887
they are forced to go further
offshore, which is quite dangerous

00:09:56.887 --> 00:09:58.180
for the Vezo.

00:09:58.306 --> 00:10:02.518
The trawlers are destroying the
habitats. They are destroying the

00:10:02.518 --> 00:10:06.105
coral reef, dragging all around and
with the fish.

00:10:06.355 --> 00:10:10.693
It's quite difficult to provide a
certain estimate of what they've

00:10:10.693 --> 00:10:15.031
been losing, but what we hear from
the elder is that there used to be

00:10:15.031 --> 00:10:20.036
bigger size of catch in terms of
fish. And now it's less and less,

00:10:20.036 --> 00:10:22.413
and most of the catch today is very small.

00:10:22.663 --> 00:10:27.877
There's a big impact for the Vezo,
especially on the livelihoods and

00:10:28.044 --> 00:10:29.420
the food security issue.

00:10:30.004 --> 00:10:32.465
Marilyn: You know, some of our
elders have talked about going out

00:10:32.465 --> 00:10:37.595
on to the fishing grounds that
there are so many boats out on the

00:10:37.595 --> 00:10:41.182
fishing grounds, at night time it
looked like a virtual city

00:10:41.599 --> 00:10:42.391
out on the ocean.

00:10:42.767 --> 00:10:47.938
Our communities can no longer
access salmon as a reliable and

00:10:47.938 --> 00:10:49.398
abundant food source.

00:10:49.398 --> 00:10:52.151
We do a lot of our own research here.

00:10:52.443 --> 00:10:59.116
Our nation has experienced a
decline up to 85% in the salmon,

00:10:59.533 --> 00:11:03.537
and that has had devastating
effects, you know, on our community.

00:11:03.788 --> 00:11:10.044
We attribute this decline to
fishing. And that includes sports

00:11:10.044 --> 00:11:13.923
and recreational. It also includes
other human activities as well.

00:11:14.006 --> 00:11:17.218
That, you know, includes the
effects of climate change.

00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:22.098
We really feel that, you know,
we're, you know, 10, 20 years

00:11:22.098 --> 00:11:23.724
beyond a crisis.

00:11:27.144 --> 00:11:30.022
Riley: We reached out to Canada's
Department of Fisheries and Oceans

00:11:30.022 --> 00:11:31.232
for comment on this story.

00:11:31.524 --> 00:11:34.402
They're aware of the significant
decline in salmon along the Central

00:11:34.402 --> 00:11:39.031
Coast, and have launched a $647.1
million effort to stabilize and

00:11:39.031 --> 00:11:39.990
rebuild stocks.

00:11:40.491 --> 00:11:41.325
Here's what they said.

00:12:12.898 --> 00:12:15.735
As coastal indigenous communities
try to combat overfishing,

00:12:16.026 --> 00:12:18.946
they’re also dealing with the issue of
climate change. And these two

00:12:18.946 --> 00:12:20.823
issues are deeply intertwined.

00:12:20.948 --> 00:12:24.034
Andrés: One of the big dangers when
you combine overfishing and climate

00:12:24.034 --> 00:12:27.246
change impacts on coastal indigenous communities is that,

00:12:27.913 --> 00:12:30.166
you know, these ways of life 
will be lost for good.

00:12:30.416 --> 00:12:32.960
You know, on the one hand, you have
these very, very large, very

00:12:32.960 --> 00:12:36.255
globalized commercial fishing
fleets that are just following fish

00:12:36.255 --> 00:12:37.423
wherever they're moving to.

00:12:37.757 --> 00:12:40.342
And then you have these habitat
impacts of climate change.

00:12:40.968 --> 00:12:42.762
Rachel: We're messing with food
chains, we're messing with

00:12:42.762 --> 00:12:48.225
habitats, we are releasing so much
gas into the atmosphere.

00:12:48.517 --> 00:12:51.395
And now we're starting to see
significant impacts on our oceans,

00:12:51.395 --> 00:12:53.647
we're starting to see reductions 
in fish stocks.

00:12:53.898 --> 00:12:58.903
Andrés: You get impacts on
mangroves, on coral reefs, on sea ice.

00:12:58.903 --> 00:13:01.405
And those are places where
indigenous peoples

00:13:01.572 --> 00:13:03.491
disproportionately live around the world.

00:13:03.908 --> 00:13:07.661
In a lot of places, just because of
colonial histories, many of these

00:13:07.661 --> 00:13:10.664
indigenous peoples' rights 
are tied to places.

00:13:10.998 --> 00:13:14.418
They're even kind of legally, in
some ways, bound to the place where

00:13:14.418 --> 00:13:16.796
they are. And so it's very
difficult for them to leave.

00:13:17.171 --> 00:13:20.758
You know, when people aren't able
to fish, they have to move

00:13:20.758 --> 00:13:24.345
outside of their communities, it
doesn't take long for the language

00:13:24.345 --> 00:13:27.181
to be lost, for the 
knowledge to be lost.

00:13:27.431 --> 00:13:29.141
And that is a loss of culture.

00:13:29.809 --> 00:13:33.229
Marilyn: It's a part of our DNA,
it's part of who we are. And if we

00:13:33.229 --> 00:13:38.025
cannot access that, we will lose
part of that identity of what we

00:13:38.025 --> 00:13:41.737
have, which has sustained us 
since time immemorial.

00:13:41.779 --> 00:13:44.782
Dresy: What the Vezo will do is to
adapt themselves on the changing

00:13:44.782 --> 00:13:49.912
environment. That's why they need
support from every stakeholder

00:13:49.912 --> 00:13:53.082
to ensure that they can 
keep their activities.

00:13:53.290 --> 00:13:56.794
Andrés: Any of the things that we
can do to help starts with granting

00:13:56.794 --> 00:13:59.964
them more sovereignty over their
resources, granting them the power

00:13:59.964 --> 00:14:03.551
to decide what they're using, 
how they're using it.

00:14:04.051 --> 00:14:06.720
Vatosoa: We need to make sure that
small scale fishers, local

00:14:06.720 --> 00:14:07.304
communities are involved and
consulted in big decisions, in high

00:14:07.304 --> 00:14:11.976
communities are involved and
consulted in big decisions, in high

00:14:11.976 --> 00:14:15.938
level decisions regarding fishing
agreements or national policy.

00:14:16.021 --> 00:14:19.859
There is the concept of locally
managed marine areas, which is a

00:14:19.859 --> 00:14:22.903
concept that was developed in the
Pacific in the 90s.

00:14:23.195 --> 00:14:26.532
One of the biggest issues of the
LMMA managers in Madagascar is the

00:14:26.532 --> 00:14:30.703
fact that they are issolated, they
are marginalized, they do not have

00:14:30.703 --> 00:14:33.247
any means of communication.

00:14:33.497 --> 00:14:36.625
So that is the reason why those
small scale fishers took the

00:14:36.625 --> 00:14:39.962
decision to create the national
network in 2012.

00:14:40.379 --> 00:14:46.802
Dresy: Currently, it's around 200 and 
more communities around the islands.

00:14:46.927 --> 00:14:50.764
This is to help all those
communities to join their voice and

00:14:50.764 --> 00:14:51.390
to be heard.

00:14:51.974 --> 00:14:56.270
Andrés: At that point they become a
legally recognized player in the

00:14:56.270 --> 00:14:58.814
management table. And that's been
something that hasn't happened

00:14:58.814 --> 00:15:02.401
historically. And it's at the root
of a lot of these issues.

00:15:02.610 --> 00:15:04.987
I do believe that commercial
fishing can coexist with

00:15:04.987 --> 00:15:07.781
traditional indigenous ways of
life. And actually, a lot of

00:15:07.781 --> 00:15:10.784
indigenous communities are fully
engaged with commercial fishing.

00:15:10.784 --> 00:15:11.827
